WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.650
Welcome back to Learn Text.js.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.740
This is chapter eight: Testing.

3
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.570
@textjs/app/testing
renders your app offscreen

4
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.117
and presses keys, into a
grid of cells in memory.

5
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:20.970
Then you read the frame back as plain
text: lastFrame returns all of it,

6
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:26.437
lines returns it row by row, and cell
gives you one cell's character and colors.

7
00:00:26.487 --> 00:00:31.340
It runs in Bun, Vitest or
Jest, with no browser involved.

8
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.340
Let's write the first test.

9
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.370
Tests sit next to the page they test.

10
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:46.863
Import render and cleanup from
@textjs/app/testing, along with the page.

11
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:50.490
afterEach cleanup unmounts
whatever a test rendered.

12
00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:55.290
render mounts the page in a grid
80 cells wide and 12 rows tall.

13
00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.170
Without options, it's 80 by 24.

14
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.370
The page fetches its deployments, so
wait for the first one to show up,

15
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.890
then check that the
frame has twelve lines.

16
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.010
To keep tests offline, stub
globalThis.fetch in a beforeEach,

17
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.340
or pass the page its data as props.

18
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.140
Next, pressing keys.

19
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.970
press takes the same key
strings as useKeymap.

20
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.890
Each call runs inside act and
flushes the frames it causes,

21
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.490
so the next assertion sees the update.

22
00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.370
Press down, and the highlight should
move to the second deployment.

23
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.490
In the next test, pressing 4
sorts by the fourth column, Build.

24
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.250
locate finds text in the frame,

25
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.940
so the 12-second build should
now sit above the 52-second one.

26
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.020
Now, run it.

27
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.530
Run the suite with bun test.

28
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:59.050
Any runner that supports async
tests works: Bun, Vitest or Jest.

29
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.450
On the lesson page, click the
pad first, so the page has focus.

30
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.530
Press Enter to run the suite.

31
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.610
All five tests pass.

32
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.170
Press F to break one, and the
failure shows the assertion,

33
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.980
and what the frame contained instead.

34
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.810
One more method, for snapshots.

35
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.250
toAnsi returns the
frame as truecolor ANSI,

36
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.690
so a snapshot records the
colors along with the text,

37
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.540
and catches color regressions too.

38
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.410
Quick check.

39
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:36.820
What does @textjs/app/testing need to run?

40
00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.730
Any test runner with async tests.

41
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.730
It renders offscreen
into a buffer of cells,

42
00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.500
so there's no browser
and no DevTools involved.

43
00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:52.300
And what does app.lastFrame() return?

44
00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.110
The frame, as plain text.

45
00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.910
Frames are trimmed on the
right and at the bottom,

46
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.560
so they make stable
assertions and snapshots.

47
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.070
Next, in chapter nine,

48
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.470
you'll run the same dashboard
full-screen in a terminal, with text run.

49
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.120
See you there.
